{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":312374,"num_turns":22,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"72c55089-b416-4b9a-a2f3-4d481771bb8d","total_cost_usd":2.3430565000000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":28,"cache_creation_input_tokens":109914,"cache_read_input_tokens":1312953,"output_tokens":23492,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":109914,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1183,"cache_read_input_tokens":124261,"cache_creation_input_tokens":921,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":921},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":28,"outputTokens":23492,"cacheReadInputTokens":1312953,"cacheCreationInputTokens":109914,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.3430565000000003,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Handed back on `diagnostics-workflow` — the unit did not begin, and no file the brief named was changed. Commit `09e5132` contains the state file only, with `turn: codex`.\n\n**What I checked and what broke.** All five claims the brief marked for checking held: the router entries, both command bodies, the research map, the consumer inventory, and the branch/checkout. But checking them falsified two premises the brief itself rests on, which the brief had not marked.\n\nThe first is the brief's framing that the two fault commands are \"left as competing routes.\" They are not. `resolve-repo-problem.md:6` carries a routing rule dated 2026-06-12, explicitly marked as superseding an earlier 2026-05-28 note, that names `/resolve-repo-problem` the triage front door and `/resolve-incident` the fix path it hands off to. `resolve-incident.md` points back the same way in three places. So one-owner routing already exists and was settled deliberately, twice. The real gap is narrower and worth naming precisely: intake has **two doors**, because the same line invites direct `/resolve-incident` use when the operator already knows the fault. That is a smaller problem than the brief assumes, and folding or retiring a command to fix it reopens a settled decision — which the Work Loop core sends to the operator, not to me.\n\nThe second is the unit's recorded mode. It says Implementation, which the core reserves for units whose build decision is settled. But the brief assigns the mechanism decision to `/develop-ai-resource`, and that command's own line 93 lists **no build**, **reuse as-is**, **defer** and **retire** as valid verdicts, with \"on no build, reuse as-is or defer — go to Step 4 and stop.\" A method that may legitimately produce no artifact cannot satisfy a completion condition that mandates \"one minimum candidate is implemented and committed.\"\n\n**The finding that would have bitten hardest.** The completion condition asks me to reconcile every directly coupled consumer, while scope excludes cross-repository changes. I found 25 symlinks to `resolve-incident.md` and 26 to `resolve-repo-problem.md`, spread across 26-plus **separate git repositories** — and every one resolves to `ai-resources/.claude/commands/`, the main checkout, not this worktree. So retiring anything would break 25 project surfaces I am forbidden to touch, and nothing committed on this branch reaches any consumer until it merges into the main checkout. The before/after routing evidence the brief requires simply cannot be produced from here.\n\n**Four real defects, recorded as deferrals and left alone.** The most concrete two: the triage-to-fix bridge is declared but not wired — `/resolve-repo-problem` emits a bridge citing a notes path, and I searched `resolve-incident.md` for `NOTES_PATH`, `notes path` and `triage notes` with no match, so it re-investigates instead of reading the notes. And `/resolve-incident` aborts at all 25 project sites, because Step 1 looks for `ai-resources/` *under* the repo root while the project repos are its siblings. Both reproduce defects the research map predicted.\n\nI suggested to Codex in `## Next action` that fixing those two would make the existing two-command path work as already documented, without reopening the 2026-06-12 decision or touching a structural change class. That is a much cheaper route to the brief's actual goal than building a new capability.","ttft_ms":2217,"ttft_stream_ms":1367,"time_to_request_ms":32,"type":"result","duration_ms":316182,"uuid":"19d332ea-9c71-4747-bb62-ddda28dfddd3"}
